WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.510
Inside a vessel, platelets are ready to respond when the vessel wall is injured.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.300
Activation releases arachidonic acid.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.400
COX-1 starts a chain that produces thromboxane A2,

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:17.450
a local signal that amplifies platelet recruitment and aggregation.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.970
Aspirin transfers an acetyl group to COX-1’s active site,

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:26.990
modifying a key serine and irreversibly reducing cyclooxygenase activity.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.120
With less thromboxane A2 produced,

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:32.220
the amplification loop becomes quieter.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:37.690
The effect lasts for the life of the platelet.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.560
This film shows one molecular pathway—not every route to clot formation,

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:46.710
and not a clinical outcome.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:53.720
Aspirin does not eliminate platelet activation or remove an existing clot.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.630
Independent concept study.
